E005 | Docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy frontmatter (Critical → P0) per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n56\t\n57\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n58\t\n59\t## Health Warnings\n60\t\n61\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. The decomposition proceeded with available information; per-epic ACs were derived from the HLRs and problem statement.\n62\t- **Team assignments are inferred, not stated**: KServe ownership (RHOAI Serving) is taken from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`. The Platform Operator team (assigned \"RHOAI Platform\") and the documentation team (assigned \"RHOAI Documentation\") are assumptions — the strategy names no owners. Human verification recommended before scheduling.\n63\t- **Component mapping — \"serving data plane\"**: the strategy's Affected Components list names only KServe and Platform Operator, but the problem statement and HLR-1 (\"all serving-stack images\") explicitly put the serving data plane in scope. The data-plane images are the model-execution runtimes, mapped to the canonical component **Serving Runtimes** (corroborated by `kserve.md`, which lists Serving Runtimes — vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit — as KServe's model-execution dependency). Flagged for the strategy author to confirm the Affected Components list is under-specified.\n64\t- **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file**: it cannot be corroborated as in-platform from `.context/architecture-context/` (only `kserve.md` exists). It is treated as an existing platform component (it is in the canonical Jira component list and the strategy treats it as an existing affected component, not a greenfield provisioning effort), so no new-component onboarding chain was generated. Its build/attestation pipeline posture is unverified — scored 0 on architecture claims and repo access; verify before relying on attestation output from its pipeline.\n65\t- **External gates are schedule risks, not acceptance criteria**: the accredited-lab turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security-review-board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. They are recorded as Risks/Notes on the evidence-package epic; its acceptance criteria sit on the deliverable we control (package completeness, submission, triage), not on the certification outcome or lab timing.\n66\t- **All epics are P0 — informational, not an inversion**: HLR-1 and HLR-2 are P0; HLR-3 (P1) is bundled per-surface into the P0 remediation epics (see traceability matrix); HLR-4 (P2) maps to the docs epic, which is P0 only via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0). No epic carries a priority below the work it enables.\n67\t- **No Investigation epics**: the strategy states \"None on scope\" for open questions, and no risks/assumptions/conditional ADRs are recorded. The per-image crypto audit (which images link which non-validated libraries) is a validation of assumptions resolved inside each remediation epic — an implementation detail, not a genuine unknown that changes which epics exist.\n68\t\n69\t## Tiered Delivery\n70\t\n71\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing is the accredited-lab certification (six-to-nine-month historical turnaround, review-board cadence in series). Partial value is deliverable without it:\n72\t\n73\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001, E002, E003 — the serving stack runs correctly on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters with validated crypto (HLR-1) and CI enforces FIPS regressions (HLR-3). Shippable regardless of certification timing.\n74\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E004 — the evidence package is assembled from Tier 1 outputs and submitted through the security review board to the certification lab (HLR-2). The engineering work (assembly, submis