{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95634c75a330d4b5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\ttitle: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n4\tstatus: Refinement Done\n5\tpriority: Critical\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- refined\n8\t- compliance\n9\tlinks: []\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## Problem Statement\n13\t\n14\tPublic-sector deals require the model serving stack to run on FIPS-enabled\n15\tOpenShift clusters using validated cryptographic modules. Today KServe\n16\tcomponents and the serving data plane link non-validated crypto libraries in\n17\tseveral images, and we have no compliance evidence package. Certification\n18\tinvolves an external accredited laboratory (historical turnaround: six to nine\n19\tmonths) and mandatory security review board sign-off at defined milestones —\n20\tthese human and third-party gates dominate the schedule regardless of\n21\tengineering speed.\n22\t\n23\t## High-Level Requirements\n24\t\n25\t| ID | Priority | Requirement |\n26\t|-------|----------|-------------|\n27\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All serving-stack images run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules (no non-validated crypto in the request path). |\n28\t| HLR-2 | P0 | A compliance evidence package (module inventory, build attestations, test results) is assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab. |\n29\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI enforces FIPS regressions: a gating check fails any serving-stack build that reintroduces non-validated crypto usage. |\n30\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites. |\n31\t\n32\t## Affected Components\n33\t\n34\t- KServe\n35\t- Platform Operator\n36\t\n37\t## Out of Scope\n38\t\n39\t- FIPS posture for workbenches, pipelines, and training (separate strategies)\n40\t- Common Criteria certification\n41\t\n42\t## Open Questions\n43\t\n44\tNone on scope — but note that lab scheduling and review-board cadence are\n45\texternally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort.\n46\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a33364966471fe34","agent_id":"af492226073e666b9","session_id":"c8475876-08d1-4b69-838f-3e562b4843af","uuid":"5bbae188-bcfa-44a7-acd5-a97db5bfcf1f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:28:30.456Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-95634c75a330d4b5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9013"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-824a173e34194154","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013 (FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS 140-3 crypto remediation for KServe component images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 crypto remediation for serving-runtime data-plane images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS 140-3 crypto remediation for the Platform Operator image | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Compliance evidence package assembly and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS support posture and cluster-prerequisite documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n21\t\n22\t## Dependency DAG\n23\t\n24\t```mermaid\n25\tgraph TD\n26\t    E001[\"E001 KServe image FIPS remediation\"] --> E004[\"E004 Evidence package + certification submission\"]\n27\t    E002[\"E002 Serving-runtime image FIPS remediation\"] --> E004\n28\t    E003[\"E003 Platform Operator image FIPS remediation\"] --> E004\n29\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 FIPS documentation\"]\n30\t    E002 --> E005\n31\t    E003 --> E005\n32\t    E004 --> E005\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tArrows point from dependency to dependent: E001/E002/E003 must complete before E004; all\n36\tImplementation epics must complete before the docs epic E005 (Rule 11).\n37\t\n38\t## DAG Justification\n39\t\n40\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n41\t|------|------|-----------|\n42\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (producer → consumer) | The evidence package consumes the KServe module inventory, build attestations, and test results produced by the KServe remediation; the content does not exist until the remediation lands. |\n43\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (producer → consumer) | Same: evidence package aggregates the serving-runtime data-plane inventory, attestations, and test results. |\n44\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (producer → consumer) | Same: evidence package aggregates the Platform Operator inventory, attestations, and test results. |\n45\t| E001/E002/E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation states the FIPS support posture that results from the remediation; blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n46\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs must describe the certified posture and submission outcome; the evidence epic is an Implementation epic and therefore blocks the docs epic. |\n47\t| No edges among E001, E002, E003 | Rules 14/15 (parallel) | Different components/repos, no shared artifacts — the three remediation streams run in parallel. |\n48\t\n49\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n50\t\n51\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n52\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n53\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002, E003 | \"All serving-stack images\" divided per surface: KServe control-plane images (E001), serving-runtime data-plane images (E002), Platform Operator image (E003). Full coverage of the serving stack. |\n54\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E004 | Evidence package assembly and submission through the security review board to the certification lab. |\n55\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E001 (bundled), E002 (bundled), E003 (bundled) | Bundled into each remediation epic: the gating FIPS-regression CI check covers exactly the images that epic converts — the enforcement deliverable is divided per surface (each epic authors and verifies the check for its own images), so a standalone CI-check epic would be a work-type extraction auditing the same surfaces twice and is not separately schedulable. |\n56\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | Docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy frontmatter (Critical → P0) per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n57\t\n58\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n59\t\n60\t## Health Warnings\n61\t\n62\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. The decomposition proceeded with available information; per-epic ACs were derived from the HLRs and problem statement.\n63\t- **Team assignments are inferred, not stated**: KServe ownership (RHOAI Serving) is taken from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`. The Platform Operator team (assigned \"RHOAI Platform\") and the documentation team (assigned \"RHOAI Documentation\") are assumptions — the strategy names no owners. Human verification recommended before scheduling.\n64\t- **Component mapping — \"serving data plane\"**: the strategy's Affected Components list names only KServe and Platform Operator, but the problem statement and HLR-1 (\"all serving-stack images\") explicitly put the serving data plane in scope. The data-plane images are the model-execution runtimes, mapped to the canonical component **Serving Runtimes** (corroborated by `kserve.md`, which lists Serving Runtimes — vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit — as KServe's model-execution dependency). Flagged for the strategy author to confirm the Affected Components list is under-specified.\n65\t- **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file**: it cannot be corroborated as in-platform from `.context/architecture-context/` (only `kserve.md` exists). It is treated as an existing platform component (it is in the canonical Jira component list and the strategy treats it as an existing affected component, not a greenfield provisioning effort), so no new-component onboarding chain was generated. Its build/attestation pipeline posture is unverified — scored 0 on architecture claims and repo access; verify before relying on attestation output from its pipeline.\n66\t- **External gates are schedule risks, not acceptance criteria**: the accredited-lab turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security-review-board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. They are recorded as Risks/Notes on the evidence-package epic; its acceptance criteria sit on the deliverable we control (package completeness, submission, triage), not on the certification outcome or lab timing.\n67\t- **All epics are P0 — informational, not an inversion**: HLR-1 and HLR-2 are P0; HLR-3 (P1) is bundled per-surface into the P0 remediation epics (see traceability matrix); HLR-4 (P2) maps to the docs epic, which is P0 only via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0). No epic carries a priority below the work it enables.\n68\t- **E002 is plausibly oversized — kept whole by design**: E002 spans three runtime families (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) and is estimated at roughly three to four engineer-weeks (per-image crypto audit, remediation, FIPS-cluster verification, and the regression gate, times three families) — above the ~2-week guideline. Why not split: the epic boundary rule is the component/team tuple, and all three runtime families belong to the same canonical component (Serving Runtimes) and the same team (RHOAI Serving); every image follows the identical audit → remediate → verify → gate pattern, shares one FIPS-regression check harness and one set of evidence-package input formats, so per-runtime epics would be three same-tuple epics auditing one surface three times. Conditional re-split trigger: if the per-image crypto audits show that any single runtime family needs a base-image change or upstream library replacement estimated at more than one week on its own, split E002 into per-runtime-family epics (new IDs continuing the sequence) along that seam — the audit itself is the natural split point because it produces the per-family effort data. E004's dependency on E002 then moves to whichever successor epic covers the slowest family.\n69\t- **No Investigation epics**: the strategy states \"None on scope\" for open questions, and no risks/assumptions/conditional ADRs are recorded. The per-image crypto audit (which images link which non-validated libraries) is a validation of assumptions resolved inside each remediation epic — an implementation detail, not a genuine unknown that changes which epics exist.\n70\t\n71\t## Tiered Delivery\n72\t\n73\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing is the accredited-lab certification (six-to-nine-month historical turnaround, review-board cadence in series). Partial value is deliverable without it:\n74\t\n75\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001, E002, E003 — the serving stack runs correctly on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters with validated crypto (HLR-1) and CI enforces FIPS regressions (HLR-3). Shippable regardless of certification timing.\n76\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E004 — the evidence package is assembled from Tier 1 outputs and submitted through the security review board to the certification lab (HLR-2). The engineering work (assembly, submission, triage) is controllable; certification completion is gated by external lab and review-board timing. E005 (documentation) follows all Implementation epics per Rule 11.\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a33364966471fe34","agent_id":"af492226073e666b9","session_id":"c8475876-08d1-4b69-838f-3e562b4843af","uuid":"ceef2d19-df89-4e73-821a-baea5b5a73c4","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:28:30.457Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-824a173e34194154","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9013"}
